<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gochang, Hwasun and Ganghwa Dolmen Sites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Korean dolmens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gochang, Hwasun and Ganghwa Dolmen Sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> hold more than 1,000 stone dolmens, used as grave markers and for rituals during the first millennium BCE. The Korean Peninsula has over 35,000 dolmens, about 40% of all in the world. They mark the graves of the ruling elite, and pottery, comma-shaped jewels, bronzes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> have been excavated from them. Dolmens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> date from the seventh century BCE, at places such as Gochang, until around the third century BCE. Excavation began in 1965, and the sites were made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gochang-hwasun-and-ganghwa-dolmen-sites.jpg" alt="Gochang, Hwasun and Ganghwa Dolmen Sites" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taewangkorea · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dolmens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> are generally classed in two types: the table or northern type, in which four stones form the walls of a box capped by a stone laid on top, and the go-board or southern type, with underground burial and stones supporting the capstone. The Gochang site, known as the Jungnim-ri dolmens and centred on Maesan village in North Jeolla province, holds the largest and most varied group. Its dolmens stand at the foot of hills at altitudes of 15 to 50 metres, with capstones of 1 to 5.8 metres that may weigh up to 225 tons, and 442 have been documented. The group is believed to date from around the seventh century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Hwasun site lies in the valleys around the Jiseokgang River and is marked by 596 dolmens in a small area, among them the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> at Daesin-ri, 7.3 metres long and 280 tons in weight. The Ganghwa dolmens stand on the slopes of mountains on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a>, higher than the others. They are believed to be the earliest, since the groups at Bugeun-ri and Gocheon-ri resemble early dolmens, though this has not been conclusively proved. The notable Ganghwa dolmen is a table-type stone measuring 2.6 by 7.1 by 5.5 metres, resting on only two supporting stones.</p><p>Three theories exist for the origin of Korean dolmens: transmission by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> culture, transmission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and a local Korean origin. The second is weakened because dolmens there are much less concentrated than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Whether the dolmens were only tombs has been debated. Complete human bones found at the Hwangseok-ri dolmen in Jecheon in 1967 confirmed their use for burial, but some argue that table dolmens also served as altars, and unusually large ones may have displayed the authority of the group that built them. Excavation at the sites began in 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dolmens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> are concentrated in South Jeolla Province, which has 10,068, with 1,597 in North Jeolla. Many in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> area were lost to urban development, including those at Wonji-dong found with artefacts in 1984. The Gochang dolmen site is listed as Historic Site No. 391, the Hwasun site as No. 410 and the Ganghwa dolmen as No. 137. The three sites were registered together as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> at the 24th session of the World Heritage Committee in Cairns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, in late 2000, and the Korean government has carried out a programme of inventory and preservation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gochang, Hwasun and Ganghwa Dolmen Sites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
